  "mechanisms": [
    "The paper initially hypothesized that high density acts as an aversive stimulus, raising drive level and affecting performance on simple (facilitation) and complex (interference) tasks, similar to social facilitation effects. However, the null findings led them to conclude that density per se does not function as an ordinary aversive, arousing stimulus in their experimental conditions.",
    "The authors suggest that any effects of high density might be more complicated, primarily affecting social behaviors and interactions among people, rather than individual task performance, and would be determined by specific conditions, individual reactions, and initial relations among individuals."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Sex of participants",
    "Motivation level (bonus vs. no bonus)",
    "Task complexity (simple vs. complex)",
    "Temporal factors (early vs. late performance, first vs. second day)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The research suggests that high density per se, when physical discomfort and other negative consequences are controlled, does not inherently produce a decrement in individual task performance. Therefore, if logistical problems of high density can be solved, it may not be inherently 'evil' and could even be encouraged or allowed rather than discouraged, given a particular population."
  ],
